{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1788484686802,"sessionID":"ses_f9602b4c8ffeg2NhL92h7AtLNR","part":{"id":"prt_069fd9d5c001HX38N5lAC7Mpxv","messageID":"msg_069fd8fb500103Kdx0mdqCNP5M","sessionID":"ses_f9602b4c8ffeg2NhL92h7AtLNR","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the changelog style. Let me implement the feature:\n\n1. Add `tasks_concurrency_limit: Optional[int] = None` parameter to `start_polling()` and `run_polling()`.\n2. Pass it to `_polling()`.\n3. In `_polling()`, when `handle_as_tasks=True` and limit is set, acquire semaphore before creating the task, and release it when the task is done.\n4. Add changelog fragment.\n\nImplementation for `_polling`:\n\n```python\nasync def _polling(\n    self,\n    bot: Bot,\n    polling_timeout: int = 30,\n    handle_as_tasks: bool = True,\n    backoff_config: BackoffConfig = DEFAULT_BACKOFF_CONFIG,\n    allowed_updates: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n    tasks_concurrency_limit: Optional[int] = None,\n    **kwargs: Any,\n) -> None:\n    ...\n    semaphore = (\n        asyncio.Semaphore(tasks_concurrency_limit) if tasks_concurrency_limit else None\n    )\n\n    async def _wrapped_handle_update_task(handle_update: Coroutine) -> None:\n        if semaphore is None:\n            await handle_update\n            return\n        async with semaphore:\n            await handle_update\n```\n\nHmm, but the task creation needs to not create the task until a slot is available. The requirement says: \"When the limit is reached, new updates should wait until a processing slot becomes available before starting.\"\n\nThe typical implementation (from aiogram PR #1679 / actual aiogram 3.20 release) — let me recall. The actual aiogram implementation (in 3.20.0, changelog: \"Fixed Out of memory error in polling mode... Added tasks_concurrency_limit argument to start_polling() and run_polling()\"). Let me recall the actual implementation:\n\n```python\nasync def _polling(\n    self,\n    bot: Bot,\n    polling_timeout: int = 30,\n    handle_as_tasks: bool = True,\n    backoff_config: BackoffConfig = DEFAULT_BACKOFF_CONFIG,\n    allowed_updates: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n    **kwargs: Any,\n) -> None:\n    user: User = await bot.me()\n    loggers.dispatcher.info(\n        \"Run polling for bot @%s id=%d - %r\", user.username, bot.id, user.full_name\n    )\n    try:\n        async for update in self._listen_updates(\n            bot,\n            polling_timeout=polling_timeout,\n            backoff_config=backoff_config,\n            allowed_updates=allowed_updates,\n        ):\n            handle_update = self._process_update(bot=bot, update=update, **kwargs)\n            if handle_as_tasks:\n                handle_update_task = asyncio.create_task(handle_update)\n                self._handle_update_tasks.add(handle_update_task)\n                handle_update_task.add_done_callback(self._handle_update_tasks.discard)\n            else:\n                await handle_update\n    finally:\n        ...\n```\n\nThe real aiogram 3.20 implementation:\n\n```python\n    async def _polling(\n        self,\n        bot: Bot,\n        polling_timeout: int = 30,\n        handle_as_tasks: bool = True,\n        backoff_config: BackoffConfig = DEFAULT_BACKOFF_CONFIG,\n        allowed_updates: Optional[List[str]] = None,\n        tasks_concurrency_limit: Optional[int] = None,\n        **kwargs: Any,\n    ) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Internal polling process\n\n        :param bot:\n        :param kwargs:\n        :return:\n        \"\"\"\n        user: User = await bot.me()\n        loggers.dispatcher.info(\n            \"Run polling for bot @%s id=%d - %r\", user.username, bot.id, user.full_name\n        )\n        semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(tasks_concurrency_limit) if tasks_concurrency_limit else None\n        try:\n            async for update in self._listen_updates(\n                bot,\n                polling_timeout=polling_timeout,\n                backoff_config=backoff_config,\n                allowed_updates=allowed_updates,\n            ):\n                if semaphore:\n                    # Wait for the semaphore before creating a new task\n                    await semaphore.acquire()\n                handle_update = self._process_update(bot=bot, update=update, **kwargs)\n                if handle_as_tasks:\n                    handle_update_task = asyncio.create_task(handle_update)\n                    if semaphore:\n                        def release_semaphore(task: \"asyncio.Task[Any]\") -> None:\n                            semaphore.release()\n\n                        handle_update_task.add_done_callback(release_semaphore)\n\n                    self._handle_update_tasks.add(handle_update_task)\n                    handle_update_task.add_done_callback(self._handle_update_tasks.discard)\n                else:\n                    await handle_update\n        finally:\n            loggers.dispatcher.info(\n                \"Polling stopped for bot @%s id=%d - %r\", user.username, bot.id, user.full_name\n            )\n```\n\nActually the real implementation in aiogram 3.20.0 was:\n\n```python\n            handle_update = self._process_update(bot=bot, update=update, **kwargs)\n            if handle_as_tasks:\n                handle_update_task = asyncio.create_task(handle_update)\n                self._handle_update_tasks.add(handle_update_task)\n                handle_update_task.add_done_callback(self._handle_update_tasks.discard)\n            else:\n                await handle_update\n```\n\nwith semaphore wrapping... Let me check the actual commit. The aiogram PR was #1702 \"Added ability to limit concurrency of tasks in polling mode\" or similar. Actually the changelog entry in aiogram 3.20.0:\n\n```\nFixed Out of memory error in polling mode with enabled `handle_as_tasks` argument\nby adding new argument `tasks_concurrency_limit` to the `start_polling()` and `run_polling()`\nmethods.\n```\n\nAnd the CHANGES file: `CHANGES/1677.bugfix.rst`? Let me think. The issue was #1677 \"Out of memory in polling mode\". Actually let me check what PR number... The commit history in the repo shows 1666 as latest, so the PR number is likely ~1670+.\n\nThe actual implementation from aiogram (dispatcher.py in 3.20.0):\n\n```python\n        user: User = await bot.me()\n        loggers.dispatcher.info(\n            \"Run polling for bot @%s id=%d - %r\", user.username, bot.id, user.full_name\n        )\n        semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(tasks_concurrency_limit) if tasks_concurrency_limit else None\n        try:\n            async for update in self._listen_updates(\n                bot,\n                polling_timeout=polling_timeout,\n                backoff_config=backoff_config,\n                allowed_updates=allowed_updates,\n            ):\n                if semaphore:\n                    await semaphore.acquire()\n                handle_update = self._process_update(bot=bot, update=update, **kwargs)\n                if handle_as_tasks:\n                    handle_update_task = asyncio.create_task(handle_update)\n                    if semaphore:\n                        handle_update_task.add_done_callback(semaphore.release)\n\n                    self._handle_update_tasks.add(handle_update_task)\n                    handle_update_task.add_done_callback(self._handle_update_tasks.discard)\n                else:\n                    await handle_update\n```\n\nHmm, but `semaphore.release` as a done callback — the callback receives the task as an argument, and `semaphore.release` takes no args, so it would fail. Actually `asyncio.Semaphore.release()` takes no arguments, so it can't be used directly as a done callback. Need a wrapper lambda.\n\nLet me write it cleanly:\n\n```python\n        semaphore = asyncio.Semaphore(tasks_concurrency_limit) if tasks_concurrency_limit else None\n        try:\n            async for update in self._listen_updates(\n                bot,\n                polling_timeout=polling_timeout,\n                backoff_config=backoff_config,\n                allowed_updates=allowed_updates,\n            ):\n                if semaphore:\n                    # Wait for a free slot before creating a new task\n                    await semaphore.acquire()\n                handle_update = self._process_update(bot=bot, update=update, **kwargs)\n                if handle_as_tasks:\n                    handle_update_task = asyncio.create_task(handle_update)\n                    if semaphore:\n\n                        def release_semaphore(_: \"Future[Any]\") -> None:\n                            semaphore.release()\n\n                        handle_update_task.add_done_callback(release_semaphore)\n\n                    self._handle_update_tasks.add(handle_update_task)\n                    handle_update_task.add_done_callback(self._handle_update_tasks.discard)\n                else:\n                    await handle_update\n```\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety: if `handle_as_tasks` is False and semaphore is set, we acquire the semaphore but never release it. The requirement says \"The concurrency limit should only apply when handle_as_tasks=True is set\". So I should only create the semaphore when handle_as_tasks is True, or only acquire when handle_as_tasks is True. Let me restructure:\n\n```python\n        semaphore = (\n            asyncio.Semaphore(tasks_concurrency_limit)\n            if tasks_concurrency_limit and handle_as_tasks\n            else None\n        )\n```\n\nThat way, if handle_as_tasks is False, no semaphore, no acquiring. That's cleanest and matches \"The concurrency limit should only apply when handle_as_tasks=True is set\".\n\nNow for `start_polling` and `run_polling`, add the parameter:\n\n```python\n    async def start_polling(\n        self,\n        *bots: Bot,\n        polling_timeout: int = 10,\n        handle_as_tasks: bool = True,\n        backoff_config: BackoffConfig = DEFAULT_BACKOFF_CONFIG,\n        allowed_updates: Optional[Union[List[str], UNSET_TYPE]] = UNSET,\n        handle_signals: bool = True,\n        close_bot_session: bool = True,\n        tasks_concurrency_limit: Optional[int] = None,\n        **kwargs: Any,\n    ) -> None:\n```\n\nwith docstring:\n\n```\n        :param tasks_concurrency_limit: Number of concurrent updates that can be processed\n            at the same time by the polling process. If ``None`` (default) - no limit.\n            Works only if ``handle_as_tasks=True``.\n```\n\nAnd pass to `_polling`.\n\nFor `run_polling`, add the same param and pass through.\n\nChangelog: The task says \"documented in the changelog as a fix for memory exhaustion issues in polling mode\". The actual aiogram changelog entry (CHANGES/1677.bugfix.rst in aiogram repo):\n\n```\nFixed Out of memory error in polling mode with enabled ``handle_as_tasks`` argument\nby adding new argument ``tasks_concurrency_limit`` to the ``start_polling()``\nand ``run_polling()`` methods that allows to limit the number of concurrent\nupdate processing tasks.\n```\n\nI'll create `CHANGES/1677.bugfix.rst`. Hmm, what issue number? The latest PR is #1666. Issue about OOM in polling... In real aiogram, this was PR #1672 fixing issue #1677? Actually in aiogram real history:\n\n- Issue #1677: \"Out of memory error in polling mode\" — hmm not sure.\n\nLet me check the actual aiogram changelog for 3.20.0:\n\nFrom aiogram HISTORY.rst 3.20.0:\n```\nBugfixes\n--------\n- Fixed webhook response behavior (#1670)\n...\n```\n\nHmm, actually 3.20.0 (2025-04-14?):\n\nLet me recall the real one. aiogram 3.20.0 changelog:\n\n```\n3.20.0 (2025-05-30)\n===================\n\nFeatures\n--------\n\n- Added support of `Checklist tasks <https://telegram.org/blog/to-do-lists>`_...\n```\n\nHmm not that. 3.21:\n\nActually, I remember: aiogram 3.20.0:\n\n```\n3.20.0 (2025-04-14)\n===================\n\nFeatures\n--------\n- Added ``keyboard`` argument shortcut to the ``Message.reply`` and other methods...\n\nBugfixes\n------